The success path speaks too, because the question is no longer why a read failed.

trustd answers exactly ONE request and then goes deaf, while staying alive at the same pid. The
daemon side is measurably innocent: its channel shows a read reply that CARRIES AN EVENT for the
second request as well as the first. libxpc is innocent too: a trace above the branching in
dispatch_mach_handler shows the listener channel being handed nothing at all for that second
request. So the event is lost between the two, and only a line here can say whether it got this far.

It does. Interleaved with trustd own log, in one run:

    machport copyout port=0x803: delivering an event   -> libxpc connected + received -> listener event
    machport copyout port=0xe03: delivering an event   -> libxpc connected + received -> message arrived
    machport copyout port=0x803: no event available, dropping
    machport copyout port=0xe03: no event available, dropping
    machport copyout port=0x803: delivering an event   -> NOTHING, no libxpc event at all

So the chain is: the daemon delivers, libkqueue delivers it to its caller, and LIBDISPATCH NEVER
CALLS THE HANDLER. That is where the next look goes.

The 0xdead path says so as well, because "no event available" and "an event was delivered" are
otherwise indistinguishable from outside, and the two spurious drops above are themselves worth
seeing: the daemon re-arms a notification after every read while has_events is still true.
--- a/src/linux/machport.c
+++ b/src/linux/machport.c
@@ -39,6 +39,16 @@
  * of them into an abort() -- so a Mach-port kqueue event that could not be read killed the process
  * and said nothing at all about why. CIDER_TRACE_KQUEUE makes the reason readable.
  */
+#define CIDER_MACHPORT_SAY(what, port) \
+	do { \
+		const char *cider_ = getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KQUEUE"); \
+		if (cider_ != NULL && cider_[0] != '\0') { \
+			fprintf(stderr, "CIDER_KQUEUE machport copyout port=0x%x: %s\n", \
+			        (unsigned) (port), what); \
+			fflush(stderr); \
+		} \
+	} while (0)
+
 #define CIDER_MACHPORT_FAIL(why) \
 	do { \
 		const char *cider_ = getenv("CIDER_TRACE_KQUEUE"); \
@@ -140,6 +150,7 @@
 	if (reply.header.code == 0xdead) {
 		// server indicated there was actually no event available to read right now;
 		// drop the event
+		CIDER_MACHPORT_SAY("no event available, dropping", src->kev.ident);
 		dst->filter = EVFILT_DROP;
 		return 0;
 	}
@@ -158,6 +169,15 @@
 	dst->ext[1] = reply.kev.ext[1];
 	dst->fflags = reply.kev.fflags;
 
